prompt:
Read lines from standard input; each non-empty line is an expression in reverse Polish notation. Tokens are separated by one or more spaces. A token is either an integer (an optional minus sign and digits, such as `7` or `-12`) or one of the operators `+`, `-`, `*`, `/`. Reading the tokens from left to right, an integer is pushed on a stack; an operator takes the top two values (the deeper one is the left operand: `8 3 -` is 5), and pushes the result. `/` is integer division that truncates toward zero (`-7 2 /` is -3, `7 -2 /` is -3).

For every line print its value. If something goes wrong, print `error: ` followed by the reason of the FIRST problem met while reading from left to right (nothing after it is looked at):
- `bad token T` for a token T that is neither an integer nor an operator;
- `stack underflow` for an operator when fewer than two values are on the stack;
- `division by zero` for a division whose right operand is 0.
If the tokens run out without a problem and the stack does not hold exactly one value, print `error: leftover values`. Lines that are empty or only spaces are skipped. All intermediate values stay far below 2^53 in absolute value.
